message:
TemperPaw process RSS is above 6.5 GB for 10 minutes. This is the host/version guard for projection, query, replay, or prepared-context materialization regressions before Railway starts OOM restarting the runtime.

Compare the Process Memory dashboard by host and version, then inspect OData `odata.entity_set_read` spans for large `candidate_count` or `materialized_count`, replay parity probes, and prepared context inline artifacts. The fixed `sha-72ad9b9` rollout sat around 1.3-1.4 GB; prior failing builds reached roughly 7.6-8.0 GB RSS.

@slack-temperpaw-alerts

A record of this entity type sat in a timed state past its own deadline. The kernel found its state_timeout already overdue: when an actor hydrated into a state whose timer was no longer live (ADR-0056), or when the scheduler replayed timers after a restart (ADR-0049). A timer that fires on time never counts, whether it is a poll (a run computer's Making poll every 2 s, its Ready check every 60 s), a scheduled step or a deadline, so this stays quiet under normal load: in the 30 days to 24 Oct 2026 it would have alerted twice (13:08 and 13:27 UTC on 24 Oct), both KatagamiRunComputer records left Ready while POST /katagami/pass/computer answered 503.

Check: the Temper kernel dashboard's hydration re-arm and overdue-on-replay widgets for the entity_type and state, then the records of that type in that state. One or two means records whose timers were lost; a wave right after a deploy means replay lag.

@slack-temperpaw-alerts

Dispatches are retrying: on average each one took more than 1.1 attempts over 10 minutes (1.0 means none retried), so transient contention is costing retry budget. Check mailbox saturation and actor cold-start latency before it escalates to exhausted.

This reads the average, not p95. The average is the histogram's exact sum over count. A percentile of this OpenTelemetry histogram is spread across its first bucket (0 to 5) by the Datadog Agent, so since the Agent's upgrade to 7.84.5 on 22 Oct 17:23 UTC p95 read 4.98 while every dispatch took exactly 1 attempt (max 1.00). The old p95 > 2 meant at least 5% of dispatches took 3 or more attempts, which is an average of at least 1.1.

@slack-temperpaw-alerts

LLM call p95 duration is above 150s over the last 15 minutes (warning at 120s). Normal p95 is ~100s; the slowest calls are usually image generation (wasm:openrouter_image_generate).

Open the session trace and inspect the LLM span: `gen_ai.system`, `gen_ai.request.model`, token usage, retries, and upstream provider status (OpenRouter / Anthropic / OpenAI).

@slack-temperpaw-alerts

WASM invocation p95 is above 5s for at least one trigger action. Check module execution, host calls, epoch drift, and integration timeout fallbacks before optimizing callers.

Upgrade is left out: KatagamiAppInstall.Upgrade runs once per installed app in a kernel roll, and each call waits for that app to restart in its owner's Temper (23 Oct 08:00 to 08:30: 38 calls, slowest 13.8 s). That wait is the restart, not slow module code, and the roll has its own checks.

@slack-temperpaw-alerts
